  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Goblins were first published as a playable race for dnd 5e in Volo’s Guide to Monsters (which is no longer available for purchase). Goblins have existed as a monster in Dungeons and Dragons for decades, and they appear as prominently in DnD settings as orcs or ogres."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Goblins have no subraces and no decision points in their racial traits, which\n\tmakes them very inflexible. Their ability score increases are good and they\n\thave several interesting traits, but their lack of decision points means that\n\tthey only work in builds where their traits are frequently impactful. Goblins\n\treally thrive when fighting with Dexterity-based weapons, and Nimble Escape\n\toffers the most important benefits of the Rogue’s Cunning Action so you can\n\teasily employ Rogue-style combat tactics without a class dip."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The custom origin rules and the updated version of the Goblin in Monsters of\n\tthe Multiverse do a lot to expand the Goblin’s options, and the utility of\n\tboth Fury of the Small and Nimble Escape allow the goblin to succeed in a wide\n\tvariety of classes."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
