    {
      "sectionId": "section-goblin-versions",
      "heading": "Goblin Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "goblin-versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-goblin-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Goblin has three versions. The first was published in Volo’s Guide to\n\tMonsters, and then was republished without changes in other source books like\n\tEberron: Rising from the Last War."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-goblin-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The introduction of the custom origin rules in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything\n\tgave us the second version. Allowing goblins to rearrange their ability score\n\tincreases allowed them to expand beyond Dexterity-based rogue-adjacent\n\tbuilds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-goblin-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Monsters of the Multiverse",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/motm/fantastical-races#Goblin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " updated the Goblin’s traits. They now use the new standard for ability score increases (+2/+1 or three +1s), and rebalanced Fury of the Small to reduce the damage but increase how often you can use it."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-goblins-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "heading": "Goblins in the 2024 DnD Rules",
      "sourceAnchor": "goblins-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-goblins-in-the-2024-dnd-rules-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Goblins were most recently updated in Monsters of the Multiverse, and have not yet been updated for the 2024 DnD rules. To play a Goblin in the 2024 DnD rules, ignore the Ability Score Increases described in Monsters of the Multiverse. Instead, use the Ability Score Increases provided by your Background in the 2024 rules."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-goblin-traits",
      "heading": "Goblin Traits",
      "sourceAnchor": "goblin-traits",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-goblin-traits-1",
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Ability Score Increases"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": +2/+1 is standard. The Goblin’s default ability score increases make them excellent Dexterity-based martial characters."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "strong",
                "text": "Age"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Rarely impactful."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Size"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Small."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Speed"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Standard."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Darkvision"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": In a game with a lot of dark\n\t\tdungeons, Darkvision is essential."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Fury of the Small"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": A decent damage boost a few times per day. There’s some rules nuance here, so see Fury of the Small, below."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Nimble Escape"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": A massive tactical advantage if you’re anywhere near melee."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Languages"
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ": Common+1 is standard."
              }
            ]
          ],
          "depths": [
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "itemKinds": [
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered",
            "unordered"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fury-of-the-small",
      "parentSectionId": "section-goblin-traits",
      "heading": "Fury of the Small",
      "sourceAnchor": "fury-of-the-small",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fury-of-the-small-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fury of the Small offers a limited-use damage boost against larger targets.\n\tMost creatures are medium or larger, so that’s a great option."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fury-of-the-small-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "At some point, someone is going to read Fury of the Small and think “I’m\n\tgoing to build an evoker and use it on everything in a big area damage spell”.\n\tUnfortunately, that’s not how it works. Fury of the Small applies the extra\n\tdamage to exactly one creature exactly one time, then can’t be used again\n\tuntil you complete a short or long rest. You could pick any one creature\n\tinside an AOE or one of several targets struck by magic missile, but the bonus\n\tdamage still only applies to one target."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fury-of-the-small-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Goblin published in Monsters of the Multiverse\n\tchanges the math on Fury of the Small, but the tactics are the same. Since\n\trepeated usage is no longer limited by resting, you’re free to burn multiple\n\tuses in the same encounter if necessary."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Goblin Classes (Customizable Origins and MMoM)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the next section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Nimble Escape is great for any artificer. For melee artificers, attack with\n\tBooming Blade then use Nimble Escape to move out of reach. You can even apply\n\tFury of the Small on the secondary damage from Booming Blade, which is lots of\n\tfun. This is partially achievable with Boots of the Winding Path, but since\n\tthey only allow you to return to a space you were previously in during your\n\tturn, they don’t help if you start your turn in melee."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For ranged artificers, use Nimble Escape to run away. Fury of the Small can’t\n\tbe triggered with things like the artillerist’s canons or the homunculus’s\n\tattack, but you can use with spells or attacks, and you’ll inevitably do\n\tenough of those to use Fury of the Small."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Barbarians don’t really need the damage boost from Fury of the Small, but it\n\tdoesn’t hurt. Nimble Escape isn’t particularly useful since running away isn’t\n\ta go-to option for the Barbarian."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bards don’t have a lot of direct damage options, so Fury of the Small is a\n\twelcome addition. Bards are also frail, so Nimble Escape is a useful defensive\n\toption, potentially making martial bards considerably more effective (though\n\tyou’ll still struggle due to weak weapon attacks)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Clerics have no shortage of damage options to deliver Fury of the Small, and\n\tNimble Escape allows you to safely walk through melee combat to deliver spells\n\twith touch range (cure wounds, revivify, etc.) or to get into a good position\n\tfor spells like Word of Radiance and Spirit Guardians."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fury of the Small and Nimble Escape both work during Wild Shape, making a\n\tgoblin circle of the moon druid a very interesting choice. Animals which have\n\tpowerful charge options like the Elk and the Rhinoceros can move away and\n\tcharge back into melee within the same turn, making such forms considerably\n\tmore effective. Other druid circles may still enjoy the ability to safely run\n\tout of melee without spending their Action to Disengage."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The fighter can do a lot by combining Nimble Escape with their Fighter’s\n\tabundant space for feats. Nimble Escape allows you to safely move out of and\n\tthrough enemies’ reach, making it easy to position yourself advantageously. By\n\tcombining this with feats like Polearm and Sentinel, you can repeatedly make\n\tOpportunity Attacks as enemies move to re-engage you. However, without the\n\tability to use a polearm with reach effectively (they all have the Heavy\n\ttrait), enemies will still be able to attack you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Like other characters with access to Booming Blade, an Eldritch Knight using\n\tNimble Escape in conjunction with Booming Blade is an effective way to hold\n\tenemies in place and then immediately move out of reach. Consider combining\n\tthis with options like Polearm Master and/or Sentinel and you can make\n\tyourself a serious problem."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Monk works fine, but Nimble Escape is redundant with what you can do by\n\tspending Ki, so the goblin’s traits don’t feel special or useful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fury of the Small and Nimble Escape both work fine for the Paladin, but the\n\tpaladin generally isn’t built for hit-and-run tactics and often struggles to\n\tfit feats into their build, so you can’t use similar tactics to the\n\tFighter."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Typically less durable than the Fighter, the Ranger can do a lot with Nimble\n\tEscape where normally you might require Zephyr Strike or the Mobile feat.\n\tSeveral subclasses do well with hit-and-run tactics or with rapidly moving\n\tbetween targets, such as a melee hunter with the hordebreaker feature."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Nimble Escape is completely wasted on the Rogue. Fury of the Small is nice,\n\tbut not enough to be the only novel thing that you get from your race."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A damage boost from Fury of the Small, and you’ll never get stuck in melee again thanks to Nimble Escape. Of course, you can get out of melee using spells like Misty Step."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A damage boost from Fury of the Small and you’ll never get stuck in melee again thanks to Nimble Escape. Grab Booming Blade and melee hexblade builds get very easy."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A damage boost from Fury of the Small, and you’ll never get stuck in melee again thanks to Nimble Escape. Of course, you can solve the melee problem with Misty Step or Kinetic Jaunt, so it’s not a huge gain for most wizards. The biggest beneficiary is likely the Bladesinger."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Goblin Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-classic",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-classic-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase, and even martial artificers will build around\n\tIntelligence."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Strength increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You could make a martially-inclined bard build work, but you’re going to be\n\tbad at spellcasting due to the lack of a Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Wisdom increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Wisdom increase. Fury of the Small would absolutely work while using Wild\n\tShape, so you could turn into something tiny and use it on small creatures,\n\tbut that’s not nearly enough."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Fighter is among the Goblin’s best options. Dexterity and Consitutiton\n\tare perfect for any Dexterity-based fighter builds, and with numerous attacks\n\tyou have plenty of opportunties to apply Fury of the Small. Nimble Escape\n\tmakes it easy to employ hit-and-run tactics, which can be useful for\n\ttactically positioning yourself or for playing a ranged build if you’re\n\tworried about being pulled into melee."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "An Eldritch Knight using Nimble Escape in conjunction with Booming Blade is\n\tan effective way to hold enemies in place and then immediately move out of\n\treach. Consider combining this with options like Polearm Master and/or\n\tSentinel and you can make yourself a serious problem."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Dexterity and Constitution ar great for the Monk, and unlike most small races\n\tthe Goblin still gets 30 ft. speed. Fury of the Small will be easy to apply\n\teven at low levels thanks to Martial Arts and Flurry of Blows, but Nimble\n\tEscape competes for your Bonus Action which is in high demand for the Monk."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Much like the Fighter, the Paladin can make effective use of the Goblin’s\n\ttraits. While you don’t get a Charisma increase, starting with 14 or 15\n\tCharisma is absolutely viable for the Paladin."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Arguably a better fit for the Goblin if only because there’s no redundant\n\tfeatures, the Ranger is a competent Scout and Striker, and the Goblin’s traits\n\tline up nicely to support any ranger build. Nimble Escape allows you to easily\n\tperform hit-and-run tactics or to snipe in ways which would normally require a\n\trogue dip or catsing Zephyr Strike."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Thematically an excellent option, but Nimble Escape replicates two of the\n\tthree functions of Cunning Action, so one of the Rogue’s most interesting\n\tfeatures is mostly redundant at level 1. Combining Sneak Attack and Fury of\n\tthe Small is a great combination, but remember that it’s still only once per\n\trest."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
